<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/cleveland-skyline.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/cleveland-skyline-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/cleveland-skyline.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="477" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/service-cities/">Service cities</a></li><li>Cleveland</li></ol></nav><p class="tag">Northeast Ohio · Cuyahoga County</p><h1>Cleveland, Ohio Insurance Appraiser</h1><p class="lede">Winter does most of the damage in Cleveland — ice dams, frozen pipes and basement water in homes that are often a century old. An independent insurance appraiser in Cleveland, Ohio makes sure that age is priced, not ignored.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-red" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-w" href="/contact-our-team/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Cuyahoga County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Northeast Ohio</dd></div><div><dt>Inspections</dt><dd>On site</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="snap"><div><small>County</small><b>Cuyahoga County</b></div><div><small>Region</small><b>Northeast Ohio</b></div><div class="wide"><small>Housing mix</small><span>Cleveland is a city of century homes: wood-frame up-and-down doubles, brick and stone houses in the Heights, and full basements under nearly everything.</span></div><div class="wide"><small>Storm history</small><span>The Great Blizzard of January 1978 still frames winter here, and the September 2008 remnants of Hurricane Ike brought hurricane-force gusts across Ohio.</span></div><div class="wide"><small>Local claim risks</small><ul class="chips"><li>Ice dams</li><li>Burst pipes</li><li>Lake-effect snow load</li><li>High winds</li><li>Basement backup</li></ul></div></div>
<h2>A winter-claims city</h2>
<p>Most of the country thinks of storm claims as summer events. In Cuyahoga County, the busiest stretch often runs from December to March. Cold air crossing Lake Erie feeds lake-effect snow that piles up on roofs, then a thaw sends meltwater back under the shingles at the eaves. Pipes in exterior walls freeze and split. Basements take water when snowmelt and rain overwhelm older drains. The Great Blizzard of January 1978 is the storm older residents still bring up, but ordinary winters generate a steady run of claims.</p>
<h2>Century homes and the two-family “double”</h2>
<p>Neighborhoods like Ohio City, Tremont, Detroit-Shoreway, Slavic Village and Old Brooklyn are packed with wood-frame houses from the late 1800s and early 1900s, including the classic up-and-down double with one unit stacked over the other. Lakewood and Cleveland Heights add larger brick, stucco and stone homes with plaster walls, hardwood floors and built-in woodwork. Almost every one sits over a full basement.</p>
<p>These homes push back against estimating software. Lath-and-plaster ceilings stained by an ice dam rarely patch cleanly. Old-growth trim and doors may need a custom match. In a double, water from the upper unit can damage both units, and both owners — or an owner and a tenant — may need to work out what each policy pays.</p>
<h2>Where Cleveland claims commonly stall</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ice dam claims</strong> paid for the ceiling stain but not the roof edge, insulation or wall cavity behind it.</li>
<li><strong>Frozen-pipe losses</strong> questioned on whether heat was maintained in a vacant unit or unheated space.</li>
<li><strong>Basement water</strong> limited to a sewer or drain backup endorsement with a low sublimit.</li>
<li><strong>Wind claims</strong> after lake-driven gales or events like the 2008 remnants of Hurricane Ike, scoped shingle by shingle.</li>
<li><strong>Plaster and trim</strong> priced as drywall and stock molding.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/appraisal-practice/ice-dam-frozen-pipe-appraisal/">ice dam and frozen pipe appraisal</a> page walks through how we document these winter losses, and the <a href="/appraisal-workbench/basement-backup-sublimit-calculator/">basement backup sublimit calculator</a> shows how quickly a cap can bite.</p>
<h2>Working with an insurance appraiser in Cleveland, Ohio</h2>
<p>We review the carrier’s estimate, inspect the home, and write a replacement-cost scope that respects how the house was actually built. When the difference justifies it and your policy includes an appraisal clause, we can represent you as your appraiser. From the near west side to the eastern suburbs and the lakeshore, the goal is the same: an amount of loss that reflects real Northeast Ohio repair costs, not a generic template.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Document the cold</b>For frozen-pipe claims, keep thermostat settings, utility bills and dates you were away. Insurers often ask whether heat was maintained, and many policies address that directly.</div>
<h2>Neighborhoods and nearby communities</h2><p>We inspect in and around Cleveland, including Ohio City, Tremont, Detroit-Shoreway, Slavic Village, Collinwood, Old Brooklyn, Lakewood, Cleveland Heights, and anywhere else in Cuyahoga County and the surrounding counties.</p>
<h2>Appraisal services in Cleveland</h2><ul class="cols2"><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/appraisal-demand-representation/">Appraisal Demand Representation</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/home-insurance-appraisal/">Home Insurance Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/hail-roof-appraisal/">Hail &amp; Roof Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/tornado-windstorm-appraisal/">Tornado &amp; Windstorm Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/derecho-remnant-storm-appraisal/">Derecho &amp; Hurricane Remnant Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/water-damage-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/fire-smoke-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Cleveland claim questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My Cleveland double had water come from the upstairs unit. Whose policy pays?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on who owns each unit and how each policy is written. If you own the whole double, your own policy usually responds to both units, with a tenant’s renters policy covering their belongings. Check your policy and keep separate records for each unit.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are ice dams covered in Cuyahoga County homeowners policies?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many policies cover interior water damage caused by ice dams, while excluding the cost of the ice itself or general roof wear. Disputes are usually about how far the damage extends into insulation, walls and roof decking.</p></div></details><details><summary>Why did the insurer pay so little for the plaster in my Lakewood home?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Estimates often price plaster ceilings and walls as standard drywall. If your home has lath and plaster, an appraiser can document the material and the extra labor involved so the scope matches what was actually damaged.</p></div></details></div>
